WEBVTT

NOTE On-screen visual text only; this approved video has no audio track.

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.350
Fix an illustrative training run at one hundred kilowatt-hours.

2
00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:14.200
Each later query uses 0.01 kilowatt-hours; ten thousand queries match training energy.

3
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:22.250
At twenty thousand queries, inference uses two hundred and the total reaches three hundred: volume matters.

4
00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:29.600
One hundred tasks at ten watt-hours each use one thousand in total.

5
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:36.767
Energy per task falls to five, while scenario demand rises to three hundred.

6
00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:44.433
The total becomes fifteen hundred: this is not inevitable; it depends on demand.

7
00:00:44.933 --> 00:00:51.783
IT equipment draws eight kilowatts while the whole facility draws ten.

8
00:00:51.783 --> 00:00:58.633
The remaining two power supporting infrastructure within this boundary.

9
00:00:58.633 --> 00:01:07.817
Power usage effectiveness is ten divided by eight: 1.25. It alone measures neither model quality nor useful work.

10
00:01:08.317 --> 00:01:15.167
Each unit needs two kilowatts; twelve available allow at most six.

11
00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:22.017
Eight slots exist, but cooling supports only ten kilowatts: five units.

12
00:01:22.017 --> 00:01:29.683
Combined capacity is the minimum of six, eight and five: five under these assumptions.

13
00:01:30.183 --> 00:01:37.033
Assign six hundred kilograms of embodied emissions to a fictional device.

14
00:01:37.033 --> 00:01:44.200
Across one million useful tasks that is 0.6 grams each; across half a million, 1.2.

15
00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:52.250
Allocation changes with service life and volume: it is not a direct measurement of each query.

16
00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:59.600
Keep consumption fixed at twenty kilowatt-hours.

17
00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:06.767
Illustrative factors of 0.1 and 0.5 kilograms per kilowatt-hour give two and ten kilograms.

18
00:02:06.767 --> 00:02:14.817
Boundary and factor matter: this calculation covers neither marginal emissions nor all manufacturing.

